From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 04:21:14 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: um: run AArch32 tasks (armhf, armeabi-v7a) on the
 Darwin guest

arm64 Linux with CONFIG_COMPAT runs 32-bit Arm programs in AArch32
state at EL0. The Darwin UML kernel gains the same task support, on the
shared compat infrastructure, for host engines that implement
AArch32. Until an engine does, the bridge reports no compat HWCAPs and
none of it is reachable: a 32-bit ELF file fails with ENOEXEC and
personality(PER_LINUX32) with EINVAL, as on a CPU without 32-bit EL0.

Task model
- The task keeps its state in the AArch64 register image: R0-R14 are
  x0-x14 zero-extended, pstate is SPSR_EL1's layout for an exception
  from AArch32, D0-D31 are V0-V15, FPSCR splits into fpsr and fpcr,
  TPIDRURW is the guest's tls and TPIDRURO the new bridge field
  tpidrro, with the dispatch flag DARWIN_UML_DISPATCH_A32_CP15BEN.
  exec clears tpidrro.
- ELF32 EM_ARM EABI executables load through the generic compat ELF
  loader, with COMPAT_ELF_HWCAP/HWCAP2 from the host's compat HWCAP
  imports. The kernel provides the kuser helpers page at 0xffff0000
  (CONFIG_KUSER_HELPERS) and a sigreturn page, both ordinary code.
- The compat mmap randomisation range is mmap_rnd_compat_bits, 11..16.

System calls and signals
- The EABI table comes from the generic compat list, with arm64
  Linux's wrappers for the 64-bit-argument calls. The ARM-private
  calls (cacheflush through flush_icache_remote_user_range(), set_tls)
  are in sys_compat.c. A number past the table gets the ARM-private
  treatment arm64 Linux gives it.
- The signal frames, VFP context and rt_sigreturn follow arm64 Linux's
  signal32.c, including SA_RESTORER and the sigpage trampolines.
- A breakpoint encoding raises SIGTRAP, as natively. personality()
  refuses PER_LINUX32 while no engine runs AArch32.

Observation
- ptrace presents the 18-word NT_PRSTATUS, NT_ARM_VFP and NT_ARM_TLS
  views and the arch/arm requests; the new pstate checks accept only
  user mode.
- uname reports armv8l under PER_LINUX32, and /proc/cpuinfo prints
  arm64 Linux's compat form (model name, Features from the compat
  HWCAPs); a 64-bit task sees the unchanged output. The SME
  vector-length prctls refuse AArch32 tasks.

Deprecated instructions
- The CP15 barrier is switched by abi.cp15_barrier (default 1, as
  natively).
- abi.swp (0 or 1, default 0, as natively) makes the kernel emulate SWP
  and SWPB on the engines' undefined-instruction event, as arm64 Linux
  does. The exchange is one atomic operation on the page the task sees,
  so it is atomic against exclusive pairs and atomic instructions on
  other vCPUs. An access that fails, an unaligned SWP included, raises
  SIGSEGV with the pc at the instruction; a failing condition retires
  the instruction untouched; a stepped one ends with TRAP_TRACE at the
  next pc. A pc operand, which the architecture leaves UNPREDICTABLE,
  is UNDEFINED, where arm64 Linux uses an unrelated register. Every
  emulation is counted as an emulation fault, traced as
  emulation:instruction_emulation and logged with a rate limit.
- SETEND is UNDEFINED, as on a CPU without mixed-endian EL0, and has no
  switch.

Bridge
- The topic leaves the bridge at ABI 24: an arm64 task's register tail
  is its FP and SME registers, 552 bytes, the kernel imports no SVE
  query, and the guest page size is 4096 only.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
